[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known: one issue, one fix queued. Read report by report, it
holds three problems. The queued parser fix closes the first one only.

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is not one bug. Fixing the parser leaves the other two open. Each problem now has a ticket of
its own, linked in the table.
</Callout>

## The problems in #4471

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} repo="https://github.com/synesthete/support-kb" issues={{ parser: 1, hint: 2, shift: 3 }} />

## How each report was placed

1. **Count each report once.** The ticket export comes in pages that overlap, so some reports
   appear twice. One id, one report.
2. **Read what the report says.** Almost every body describes a day and a month that swapped
   places: "booked 3 October, got an invite for 10 March". A few describe something else: a date
   that moved by one day, or one meeting that showed two different days in two time zones. The desk
   already listed those in its notes as one-day moves. They are `shift`.
3. **Let the tenant decide the swaps, not the subject or the tag.** Every tenant on file writes
   the day first. A swap that came in through booking-v3's own form is `hint`; every other swap is
   `parser` (the rule in `causes.ts`).

## parser · the shared parser reads day-first dates month first

Every tenant writes dates day first. The shared parser reads every date month first, so a date like
7/4 becomes 4 July instead of 7 April, and the booking lands in another month. This is what #4471 describes, and the queued fix is for it: read dates in the tenant's locale.

## hint · booking-v3 prints an MM/DD hint to day-first tenants

booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field for every tenant. A day-first tenant's
clients type the month first, as the hint tells them. The form then reads that field day first, the
tenant's way, and the date swaps before it ever reaches the shared parser. The parser fix does not
touch this. The fix is in the form: print the hint in the tenant's order (booking-v2 already prints
`DD/MM/YYYY`), or replace the free-text field with a date picker.

## shift · a date moves by one day with the time zone

These reports name no swap. Tuesday shows as Monday; a client in Toronto sees Monday while a client
in Lisbon sees Tuesday for the same meeting; an invite arrives before the booking was made. That
looks like a date-only value stored as a moment in one time zone and shown in another. Neither the
parser fix nor the hint fix changes it.

## Why the desk saw one

- **The subject cannot tell them apart.** The desk merges reports whose subject lines match
  ([decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject)). All three problems arrive under the same
  subject lines ("Wrong day on the invite", "Date is wrong"), so all three were merged into one ticket.
- **The intake tag is a guess.** Intake tags from the reporter's browser. The "tagged as" column
  shows how often that guess disagrees with the cause.
- **The page already showed it.** The #4471 page's own table lists `hint` as a second cause with
  "no ticket of its own", and footnotes the one-day moves under `parser`. Its words still say one
  issue.

## What this page asks of the desk

- Keep #4471 for `parser` only; `hint` and `shift` are tracked in their own tickets, linked above.
- Decision 0001 is accepted, so it stays as written. Merging on cause instead of subject would be a
  new decision record that replaces it; this page proposes it and does not write it.

